   <text>
      <body>
         <div type="letter">
                <div type="mainBody">
                    <opener>
                        <salute>Dear <name type="person" key="poun85">Pound</name>
                        </salute>
                    </opener>
                    <p>I am not absolutely sure whether your postscript is</p>
                    <p>
                        <foreign xml:lang="deu">Adresse</foreign>: Hotel Excelsior</p>
                    <p>(ie. <seg xml:id="seg1" type="note-anchor">
                            <name type="person" key="haup62">G.H</name>’s address)</seg>
                    </p>
                    <p>or</p>
                    <p>
                        <seg xml:id="seg2" type="note-anchor">
                            <foreign xml:lang="ita">adesso</foreign>
                        </seg>: Hotel Excelsior</p>
                    <p>(ie. that you have moved?)</p>
                    <p>So I enclose <seg xml:id="seg3" type="note-anchor">the letter for <name type="person" key="haup62">Hauptmann</name>
                        </seg> to you to be on the safe side as I suppose your mail is forwarded if you have moved which does not seem probable to me only possible<supplied reason="omitted">.</supplied>
                    </p>
                    <closer>
                        <signed>
                            <name type="person" key="joyc82">J.J.</name>
                        </signed>
                    </closer>
                </div>
            </div>
            <div type="notes">
                <note xml:id="note1" target="#seg1" type="commentary">JJ had sent Pound a copy of the play, <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Michael Kramer</name>, by the German social realist Gerhart Hauptmann (1862-1946), for Hauptmann to sign, and by now had received the signed book back from Pound, for which he enclosed, with the present letter, a letter of thanks to be given to Hauptmann. Hauptmann often spent the winter months at the Grand Hotel Excelsior and Casino in Rapallo, Italy. Pound had lived in Rapallo with his wife Dorothy since 1924-1925. (For additional details concerning Hauptmann and <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">Michael Kramer</name>, see <rs type="letter" key="L_70880">JJ to Pound 5 December 1937</rs>, notes.)</note>
                <note xml:id="note2" target="#seg2" type="commentary">It. “now,” “at present.” Pound had not moved from his flat at 12 via Marsala in Rapallo.</note>
                <note xml:id="note3" target="#seg3" type="commentary">Pound finished his assigned task as a special-delivery service on 15 February 1938. He wrote to “[d]ear JaYmes” on 16 February: “I deelivered yr epustle to the Venbl/ Haup yester” (Robert Spoo, “Unpublished Letters of Ezra Pound to James, Nora, and Stanislaus Joyce,” <name type="work" key="ID_MISSING">James Joyce Quarterly</name>, vol. 32, no. 3-4 [Spring-Summer 1995]: 574).</note>
            </div>
      </body>
